<section class="block">
<h2>What was announced</h2>
<p>Prime Minister Anthony Albanese announced that the government will legislate mandatory safety and security obligations for frontier AI developers, enforced by a new AI safety regulator able to impose significant penalties<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. Reporting describes six obligations covering security controls, identifying and managing serious risks, allowing independent evaluation of models, publishing safety frameworks, rapid reporting of serious incidents and adapting to updated standards; the regulator would work with the Australian AI Safety Institute<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>. The government is also considering criminal penalties for egregious failures<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>.</p>
<p>Key details are open: which companies count as frontier developers, the size of penalties and how fast incidents must be reported<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. Commentators note the list is narrower than the guardrails Australia proposed in 2024 for high-risk uses of AI more broadly<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>The trigger was a deployment problem</h2>
<p>The announcement followed a review after an OpenAI agent reached non-public material on a Services Australia Medicare statistics portal, reportedly after moving beyond the intended scope of its task<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>. Whatever the final rules say about model developers, the failure mode is familiar to anyone running agents: an agent with broad access, a loosely defined task and no hard boundary on what it may reach.</p>
<figure class="dgm">
<ol class="dgm-flow g3">
<li><b>Task</b><span>An agent is given a goal, often loosely worded</span></li>
<li><b>Access</b><span>It holds tools and network reach broader than the task needs</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Drift</b><span>Pursuing the goal, it reaches systems or data outside scope</span></li>
<li><b>Unnoticed</b><span>No control blocks it and no alert fires</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Incident</b><span>Non-public data is accessed or exposed</span></li>
<li><b>Report</b><span>Someone must work out what happened, from logs that may not exist</span></li>
</ol>
<figcaption>How agents stray. Each step is a place a control could have stopped it.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Who is affected, and how</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-lanes">
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Frontier developers</small><ol><li>Security controls</li><li class="hl">Independent evaluation</li><li>Incident reports to regulator</li></ol></div>
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Companies building on them</small><ol><li>New contract terms</li><li class="hl">Incident notices from providers</li><li>Possible service changes</li></ol></div>
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Companies deploying agents</small><ol><li>Scope and permissions</li><li class="hl">Logging and detection</li><li>Own incident process</li></ol></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Direct duties fall on developers; practical duties flow down to everyone using their models.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p><strong>Frontier developers</strong> face the direct obligations. <strong>Companies building on their models</strong> should expect them to flow down: providers will want customers' help with incident reporting and evaluation, and may change terms or features in Australia. <strong>Companies deploying agents</strong>, whatever model they use, face the same risk that triggered the rules, and regulators elsewhere are watching the same incidents.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>What to do now</h2>
<p><strong>Scope every agent to its task.</strong> Agents should hold only the tools, data and network reach the task needs, through short-lived credentials, with allowlists for the systems and sites they may contact. Central authorisation through your identity provider makes this manageable across many agents and tools; the MCP Enterprise-Managed Authorization extension is one example of that pattern<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-6">6</a></sup>.</p>
<figure class="fig"><div class="im"><img src="/assets/img/briefings/mcp-ema-comparison.webp" alt="Diagram comparing standard MCP authorisation, where the user authenticates to each server separately, with Enterprise-Managed Authorization, where one identity provider login connects all approved servers" width="1400" height="769" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>Central authorisation: one place to decide what each agent and user may reach. <span class="credit">Source: <a href="https://blog.modelcontextprotocol.io/posts/enterprise-managed-auth/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Model Context Protocol Blog, Paul Carleton</a> · CC BY 4.0</span></figcaption></figure>
<p><strong>Log and detect.</strong> Record every agent action with the agent's identity and the user it acts for, and alert when an agent reaches a system or site it has never used. Without this, you cannot report an incident accurately, to a regulator or to your own board.</p>
<p><strong>Prepare to report.</strong> Define what counts as a serious AI incident in your organisation, who decides, and how you would tell customers, providers and, where required, regulators. The rules here bind developers, but your customers will ask you the same questions.</p>
<p><strong>Update contracts.</strong> Ask AI providers how they will notify you of incidents affecting your use, what evaluation access they may request, and what changes they plan for Australia.</p>
<div class="callout"><p>Regulation will catch up with model developers. The agent you deployed last month is your responsibility today. Scope it, log it and be ready to explain what it did.</p></div>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>CTO take</h2>
<p>Australia joins a growing list of jurisdictions putting frontier AI developers under specific obligations; see <a href="/insights/india-frontier-ai-law-what-it-means/">India's frontier AI law</a> and <a href="/insights/tech-news-2026-11-11/">New York's registration rules</a>. The more useful lesson for most companies is in the trigger: agents that can reach more than their task requires will eventually do so. Treat agent scope, identity and logging as core architecture, not a compliance afterthought. See <a href="/insights/agentic-infrastructure-skills-tools-sandboxes/">agentic infrastructure</a>, the <a href="/case-studies/ai-provider-resilience-cps-230-insurer/">AI provider resilience case study</a> and the <a href="/fractional-cto-australia/">Australia page</a>.</p>
</section>

